==English==

===Etymology===
Compare {{cog|da|slubbre||to swallow, to sup up}}, and {{cog|en|slabber}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈslʌbɚ/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈslʌbə/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈslʊbə/|a=Northern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈslʊbəɹ/|a=Ireland}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈslʌbəɹ/|a=Scotland,Northern Ireland}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌbə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## To do (something) [[hastily]], [[imperfectly]], or [[sloppily]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|act=II|scene=viii|page=171|passage=[H]e {{quote-gloss|Antonio}} anſvvered, do not ſo, / '''Slubber''' not buſineſs for my ſake, Baſſanio, / But ſtay the very riping of the time}}
## {{lb|en|also|figurative}} To [[cover#Verb|cover]] or [[daub#Verb|daub]] (something) [[carelessly]] with a [[substance#Noun|substance]]; to [[stain#Verb|stain]].
##* {{RQ:Milton Of Reformation|page=42|passage=[T]here is no art that hath bin more canker'd in her principles, more ſoyl'd, and '''ſlubber'd''' vvith aphoriſming pedantry then the art of policie; {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[slobber#Verb|slobber]].
#* {{RQ:London Mutiny of the Elsinore|chapter=33|passage=It grows colder, and grayer, and penguins cry in the night, and huge amphibians moan and '''slubber'''.}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|slubberingly}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A person who, or a [[machine]] which, [[slub]]s.

===References===
* ''Oxford English Dictionary'', second edition (1989)
* ''Random House Webster's Unabridged Electronic Dictionary'' (1987-1996)

